"Use Claude’s chat search and memory to build on previous context | Claude Help Center (https://support.claude.com/en/articles/11817273-use-claude-s-chat-search-and-memory-to-build-on-previous-context)\nciteturn15view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn13view3\",\"lineno\":80}); Total lines: 412\nL0: cite0†Skip to main content L1: \nL2: cite1†API docs†docs.claude.com cite2†Release notes cite3†How to get support L3: \nL37:   * cite18†Amazon Bedrock L38: # Use Claude’s chat search and memory to build on previous context\nL39: \nL40: Updated over 2 weeks ago\nL41: \nL42: Copy for LLM\nL43: \nL44: You can prompt Claude to search through your previous conversations to find and reference relevant information in new chats. Claude can also remember context from your chats and carry it into new conversations and Cowork tasks. This article explains how chat search and memory work, what Claude does and doesn't remember, how to review and edit what's saved, and how to turn these features on or off.\nL45: Important: We have introduced an improved experience for memory and migrated users off the legacy experience. If you think Claude has forgotten something in that migration:\nL46: \nL47:   * Navigate to cite19†Settings > Memory†claude.ai and until September 9, 2026, you will see the option to export your legacy memory.\nL48: \nL49:   * Once you have exported your legacy memory, paste it back into Claude, highlighting the portion that may have been forgotten.\nL50: \nL51: * * *\nL52: ## Search past chats with Claude\nL53: \nL54: Searching past chats is available to users on paid plans (Pro, Max, Team, and Enterprise plans) on the web, Claude Desktop, and Claude Mobile apps.\nL55: You can prompt Claude to search through your previous conversations to find relevant information across sessions and reference specific details when needed. Simply ask Claude to find what you discussed before, and it will pull together the appropriate context to keep your conversation flowing. These searches use Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) and will appear as tool calls during your conversations.\nL56: ## What Claude can search\nL57: \nL58: You can prompt Claude to search conversations within these boundaries:\nL59: \nL60:   * All chats outside of projects.\nL61: \nL62:   * Individual project conversations (searches are limited to within each specific project).\nL63: ## Search and reference past chats\nL64: \nL65: Once the ability to search past chats is rolled out to your account, it will be enabled by default. Just ask Claude about your previous conversations naturally to use it, such as:\nL66: \nL67:   * \"What did we discuss about [topic]?\"\nL68: \nL69:   * \"Can you find our conversation about [subject]?\"\nL70: \nL71:   * \"Let's continue where we left off with [project].\"\nL72: \nL73: When Claude searches your previous chats, you will see this reflected in your current chat as a tool call.\nL74: ## Can I prevent Claude from searching my past chats?\nL75: \nL76: Yes, navigate to cite19†Settings > Memory†claude.ai and switch the toggle next to \"Search and reference chats\" off:\nL77: ## Can I exclude a specific past chat from searches?\nL78: \nL79: Incognito chats are available to all Claude users (free, Pro, Max, Team, and Enterprise plans). See cite20†Use incognito chats for more information.\nL80: \nL81: When starting a new chat with Claude outside of a project, you'll see a ghost icon in the upper right corner of your screen.\nL82: Clicking the ghost icon will open an incognito chat, creating a temporary conversation that isn’t saved to your chat history. Claude won’t pull information from incognito chats when searching previous conversations.\nL83: Important: If you’re using an Enterprise or Team plan account, incognito chats are included in standard data exports and follow your organization's data retention policies. You can't search past chats if your organization uses customer-managed encryption keys on an Enterprise plan because conversation content is encrypted.\nL84: \nL85: * * *\nL86: ## What is Claude's memory?\nL87: \nL88: Claude can generate memory based on your chats. With the addition of memory, Claude transforms from a stateless chat interface into a knowledgeable collaborator that builds understanding over time.\nL89: Memory is on by default for Free, Pro, and Max plans on the web, Claude Desktop, and Claude Mobile (update to the latest version of the app). On Team and Enterprise plans, owners control whether memory is available, and it's off for each member until they turn it on. Memory is shared between Chat and Claude Cowork when Cowork runs in the cloud; Cowork sessions that run locally on your computer don't use memory.\nL90: ## How does Claude’s memory work?\nL91: \nL92: In addition to searching past chats, enabling Claude's memory feature adds several capabilities.\nL93: \nL94: ### How Claude stores memory\nL95: \nL96: Claude saves memory as a set of individual topics as you chat, rather than summarizing conversations after they end. Mention that a deadline moved, and your next conversation already knows. Claude saves on its own, and you can also tell Claude to \"remember this\" to save something directly.\nL97: ### Project memory and summary\nL98: \nL99: Each project has its own separate memory space and dedicated project summary, so the context within each of your projects is focused, relevant, and separate from other projects or non-project chats.\nL100: ### One memory across chat and Claude Cowork\nL101: \nL102: What Claude remembers from your chats is available when you hand it a task in Cowork in the cloud, and what comes up in a Cowork task carries back to chat. For example, ask Cowork to draft an update for your manager, and it already knows who that is and how they like updates written.\nL103: To run a single chat or Cowork task without memory, open a new chat, click the “+” menu, and turn off \"Memory.\" Claude won't use or add to your memory in that chat or task, and your other chats and tasks aren't affected. Your memory setting is set when the task starts and can't be changed later.\nL104: \nL105: Note: Memory across Cowork and chat only works when Cowork runs in the cloud. It isn't available in Cowork sessions that run locally on your computer. Learn more about Cowork in the cloud.\nL106: ## Turn memory on or off for your account\nL107: \nL108: Note: Members of Enterprise plans can only enable this feature individually when it’s enabled by an owner for their organization. For more information, see cite21†Controls for Enterprise plan owners .\nL109: \nL110: You can toggle Claude’s memory on by navigating to cite19†Settings > Memory†claude.ai and turning on Generate memory from chats:\nL111: \nL112: If you want to disable Claude’s memory, click the toggle and you'll see two options:\nL113:   * Pause memory: Claude keeps its existing memory, including sensitive topics if you have it turned on, but won't use memory or make new memories. Conversations while memory is paused won't be added to memory if you turn it back on. If you unpause memory, both your memory and sensitive topics memory will be on.\nL114:   * Reset memory: Permanently deletes all memories including project memories. Once you select this option and click \"Reset memory,\" this cannot be undone. Upon re-enabling the feature, you’ll start from scratch and Claude will not have its previous memory.\nL115: ## Turn off memory for a single chat\nL116: \nL117: Turning off memory for a single chat is available on the web, Claude Desktop, and the latest version of Claude Mobile.\nL118: \nL119: You can leave memory on for your account and still turn it off for one chat. Claude won't use what it remembers about you in that chat, and nothing from the chat is saved to memory. Unlike an incognito chat, the chat stays in your chat history and Claude can still find it when searching past chats from your other conversations.\nL120: You need to turn memory off before you send the first message. Once the chat starts, the setting can't be changed. To change it, start a new chat.\nL121: ### On the web and Claude Desktop\nL122: \nL123:   1. Start a new chat.\nL124: \nL125:   2. Click \"+\" in the message box.\nL126: \nL127:   3. Click \"Memory\" to turn it off for this chat.\nL128: \nL129:   4. Send your first message.\nL130: \nL131: After you send your first message, a crossed-out memory icon appears next to the chat title. Chats with memory on don't show an icon.\nL132: ### On Claude Mobile\nL133: \nL134:   1. Start a new chat.\nL135: \nL136:   2. Tap \"+\" next to the message box.\nL137: \nL138:   3. Turn off the \"Memory\" switch and close the sheet.\nL139: \nL140:   4. Send your first message.\nL141: \nL142: After you send your first message, the switch stays visible in the \"+\" menu but locked, so you can check a chat's settings at any time.\nL143: This works the same way in projects. A chat started with memory off in a project doesn't use your memory or the project's memory. If you want to start the chat in voice mode, turn memory off first, then tap the voice button.\nL144: \nL145: Note: This option only turns memory off. If memory is already off for your account or your organization, or your plan doesn't include it, you won't see \"Memory\" in the \"+\" menu. It doesn't appear in incognito chats either.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nJob Application Tracker & CRM - Track your job applications and keep your job search organized (https://huntr.co/product/job-tracker)\nciteturn15view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn13view2\",\"lineno\":40}); Total lines: 248\nL0:   * Product\nL1: \nL2: Better resumes & cover letters\nL3: \nL4:   * cite0†AI Resume Builder L5:   * cite1†AI Resume Review L6:   * cite2†Resume Checker L7:   * cite3†AI Cover Lette